WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.070
The grammar is something that
we do care about, but it's not

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.480
something that should be upfront.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.220
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:14.580
Today I want to talk about the
fifth of my, uh, hierarchy of

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.460
goals and, uh, and that's grammar.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.330
Um, some people may think that, or
at least in traditional language

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.950
instruction, grammar was the first goal:

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:26.760
get it right.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.750
Uh, But before I go any further
to remember if you enjoy these

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.310
videos, um, please subscribe, click
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.460
And um, if you follow me on a podcast
service, uh, please leave a comment.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.180
We do appreciate it.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.110
So, I mean, typically we're
used to the idea that language

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.760
instruction involves grammar.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.800
It involves the teacher explaining grammar
rules, which are introduced one at a time,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:59.415
according to, you know, order that someone
decided we begin with the present tense

00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:01.425
and not the past tense, no real reason.

00:01:01.695 --> 00:01:04.365
Uh, typically in Arabic, they
begin with the past tense and

00:01:04.365 --> 00:01:05.535
then move to the present tense.

00:01:06.105 --> 00:01:11.505
Um, so there seems to be this order in
which grammar concepts are introduced

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:16.745
and then we're tested on them, or
we do exercises on them and the

00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:21.135
sort of the core element in language
instruction seems to be grammar.

00:01:21.135 --> 00:01:25.485
Everything is built around grammar, uh,
typically in these textbooks that will

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:32.265
be, um, you know, content items, items of
content, uh, to introduce new vocabulary.

00:01:32.535 --> 00:01:35.235
But together with that,
inevitably there is a concept

00:01:35.325 --> 00:01:36.765
of grammar that is introduced.

00:01:37.665 --> 00:01:43.155
To me, grammar or speaking
correctly, or correct usage as

00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:46.185
a goal it's way down the list.

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:46.725
All right.

00:01:46.755 --> 00:01:51.555
You, by now will know that I consider
acquiring words as the first goal,

00:01:51.585 --> 00:01:55.335
getting a lot of words, uh, getting
to where I can start reading in

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:57.225
the language is the second goal.

00:01:57.585 --> 00:02:02.355
The third goal is listening and of course
the reading helps you with listening.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:05.625
Cause if I just hear it, I don't
necessarily know what I'm missing.

00:02:06.645 --> 00:02:10.755
Uh, and then I get to speaking because
now I've been listening to so much

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:14.175
of the language that I have words,
I feel that I'm ready to speak.

00:02:14.865 --> 00:02:19.905
And so it's only when I start speaking
that my inaccuracy in the language

00:02:19.905 --> 00:02:25.005
becomes a problem because as long as I'm
input based, I'm listening and reading,

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:29.265
I'm requiring words, I can actually
understand fairly well what's happening.

00:02:29.265 --> 00:02:33.555
I have some concepts of the grammar,
but because I'm not producing much of

00:02:33.555 --> 00:02:35.125
the language, I'm not making mistakes.

00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:36.825
So it's a happy situation.

00:02:36.825 --> 00:02:39.555
I'm enjoying the language,
no pressure, no mistakes.

00:02:40.185 --> 00:02:43.695
Now, once I start speaking, then
of course we all would like to

00:02:43.695 --> 00:02:46.125
speak more correctly, more...

00:02:46.135 --> 00:02:49.995
the usage patterns, we want to imitate
the usage patterns of the native speaker.

00:02:50.355 --> 00:02:56.025
To some extent, if we put a lot of effort
into our listening and reading and we

00:02:56.025 --> 00:02:58.005
have lots of words, we will have a...

00:02:58.005 --> 00:03:02.385
begin to have a feel for how
the language works, but, and we

00:03:02.385 --> 00:03:03.635
will notice certain patterns.

00:03:04.005 --> 00:03:07.065
And we will notice that for example,
the verb comes at the end for example.

00:03:07.065 --> 00:03:10.395
We don't have to be told that, that
becomes pretty obvious pretty quickly.

00:03:10.515 --> 00:03:13.515
Although if we're told that upfront,
there's nothing wrong with that either.

00:03:13.785 --> 00:03:19.245
But so much of what is in grammar, at
least in the initial stages in my approach

00:03:19.245 --> 00:03:24.465
is just to be kind of vaguely aware that
these things exist in the language without

00:03:24.525 --> 00:03:26.615
necessarily expecting to remember it.

00:03:27.595 --> 00:03:30.969
But comes a point now where I'm
speaking and I always make...

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.660
struggle to get my thoughts out.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.390
And I'm not quite sure how that
structure works, even though I've

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.190
seen it, I'm not able to produce it.

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.989
And so now we really have to focus in,
on, on producing the language correctly.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.200
And so, but the advantage is if we
start focusing in on grammar after

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.395
having put enough effort into the
input we now have some idea of what the

00:03:55.395 --> 00:03:59.925
grammar explanations refer to because
we've already experienced much of it.

00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:04.395
And so we look at the grammar rule,
we still can't apply it, but I

00:04:04.395 --> 00:04:10.425
think that studying grammar after a
significant exposure to the language

00:04:10.815 --> 00:04:14.175
helps us grasp these grammar rules.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.600
And even if we forget them and we look at
them again and we look at them again and

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.870
we look it up again, slowly, our ability
to use the grammar correctly improves.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:27.330
And I don't think it's
necessary to be tested on them.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.490
I think it's enough to review the
grammar rules, try to notice them

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.780
when we're listening and reading.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.550
And I've always found that in
time if I have enough words,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.620
I'll be able to start speaking.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.104
So the grammar is something that
we do care about, but it's not

00:04:48.104 --> 00:04:49.515
something that should be upfront.

00:04:50.294 --> 00:04:55.815
And because I'm very much focused on
input based learning, I would like to

00:04:55.815 --> 00:05:03.044
see more grammar books where concepts
of grammar are introduced in the target

00:05:03.044 --> 00:05:04.544
language in Spanish and Japanese.

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:10.860
And that there is a company audio for that
lesson so that I can go away and I can

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:16.170
listen to a discussion of the subjunctive
in Spanish with a lot of examples and

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:20.219
I can listen to it and I can read it so
that it becomes a lesson like any other

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:24.810
lesson and I'm learning the grammar
rules, but in the target language, I'm

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.270
not sure if resources like that exist.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.655
Um, I've often...

00:05:30.135 --> 00:05:33.735
I haven't had that so what I've
relied upon is grammar resources

00:05:33.765 --> 00:05:35.025
that I can find on the internet.

00:05:35.025 --> 00:05:37.515
I can download a PDF of Romanian grammar.

00:05:37.725 --> 00:05:43.965
I can look up specifics of, you know,
Ukrainian noun declensions, or anything

00:05:44.025 --> 00:05:45.555
really that you need to look up.

00:05:45.915 --> 00:05:46.875
Uh, you can look it up.

00:05:47.325 --> 00:05:51.615
And, uh, certainly with verbs,
uh, at LingQ, we have conjugating

00:05:51.615 --> 00:05:52.905
dictionaries in many cases.

00:05:52.905 --> 00:05:57.164
So as you look up a form of the verb,
it'll tell you which form it is and

00:05:57.164 --> 00:06:01.424
it'll show you the other, uh, you
know, conjugations of that verb.

00:06:01.434 --> 00:06:06.885
So there's so many resources around that
help us treat grammar as something that

00:06:07.244 --> 00:06:12.734
we refer to rather than something that has
to be deliberately learned and certainly

00:06:12.734 --> 00:06:14.684
not deliberately learned upfront.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:20.804
So, and with that, I find over time,
my accuracy, it gets better and,

00:06:20.804 --> 00:06:22.135
but there will always be mistakes.

00:06:22.484 --> 00:06:25.365
And in many cases, I'll make
the same mistake week after

00:06:25.365 --> 00:06:26.415
week, month after month.

00:06:26.655 --> 00:06:29.955
And eventually these mistakes
do correct themselves.

00:06:30.405 --> 00:06:36.165
So grammar therefore or correct
usage is for me, uh, in my hierarchy

00:06:36.165 --> 00:06:40.545
of goals number five, not one, not
two, not three, not four but five.

00:06:41.385 --> 00:06:41.925
Okay.

00:06:41.985 --> 00:06:43.005
Thank you for listening.

00:06:43.245 --> 00:06:43.815
Bye for now.

